{
  "book.totem_alchemy.guide.principles": "Alchemy Principles\n\nAlchemy does not reveal material answers in advance. Put a material through a Brewing Stand or Alchemy Cauldron; the manual begins recording it only after a reaction completes.\n\nOne material may produce several effects. Each effect is rolled independently, while every bottle in the same brewing batch shares the same result set.\n\nRepeated experiments add samples, making processing-time and observed-frequency estimates progressively more reliable.",
  "book.totem_alchemy.guide.brewing_stand": "Brewing Stand\n\nThe Brewing Stand handles standard potion brewing and material research. Use potion bottles and Blaze Powder fuel as usual, then place the material you want to study in the ingredient slot.\n\nWhen a brew completes, the nearby player who began the experiment receives one material sample, its processing time, and the effects actually observed.\n\nAll three potion slots belong to one batch and therefore share the same random result set. Failed reactions can still contribute processing-time data.",
  "book.totem_alchemy.guide.cauldron": "Alchemy Cauldron\n\nPlacement: for heated cauldron recipes, place a vanilla Cauldron directly above a lit Campfire with no block between them. Fill it first when the recipe requires water.\n\nA Water Cauldron can also start potion mixing directly, or you can pour a drinkable potion into an empty or water-filled Cauldron. The first valid reaction automatically converts it into an Alchemy Cauldron.\n\nAdd materials by right-clicking or dropping them into the Cauldron as the recipe allows. Bottle liquid mixtures with a Glass Bottle. Looking at an active Alchemy Cauldron shows its reaction stage and volume.",
